Required gates are selected from the repository’s actual format, lint, typecheck, unit, integration, build, and approved security commands. A failed blocking gate cannot be overruled by a reviewer vote.

Gate evidence includes the command, exit status, relevant output, environment, and criterion it supports.

## Readiness states

Beacon keeps three independent states:

- `localReady` means ordinary repository development validation passed;
- `publicationReady` means the exact candidate SHA passed every deterministic prepublication gate;
- `externalReady` means the required real GitHub, provider, attestation, promotion, approval, verification, and rollback evidence passed.

No state implies the next one. Phase 1.5 freezes only when the completion audit reports all three true.

## Authoritative prepublication gate

`npm run ci:prepublish` is the final deterministic publication command. It binds machine-readable evidence to `HEAD` and runs the local phase audit, formatting, lint, typecheck, unit and agent tests, telemetry/eval/model checks, secret/dependency/license/workflow policy, documentation build and links, the full prebuilt browser suite, deterministic PR metadata, publication scope/evidence, changed-path classification, and `git diff --check`. It writes `publicationReady: false` and prints `PUBLICATION DENIED` when any required gate fails or is missing.

Changed-path classification selects efficient checks during iteration. It never removes a check from the final prepublication command.

The publication manifest owns the required PR sections. The same policy validates generated local metadata, the GitHub pull-request event, and the title/body read back from GitHub. GitHub workflows remain independent verifiers.

## Motion accessibility contract

With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, decorative CSS animation and transition are absent, reveal content is immediately visible, counters and progress resolve to their final values, tilt/parallax/autoplay are disabled, and automatic scrolling is not smooth. Browser tests also prove normal motion remains enabled; removing all animation is not a passing implementation.
